window.MEMO={"title":"門外漢談 AI","en":"A Layman Thinks About AI","date":"2026-11-11","author":"Howard Marks","summary":{"intro":["Marks 認為 AI 是這個時代最重大的發展，投資人沒有「不去想它」的選項；但 AI 牽涉的變數異常多（技術、算力、競爭、受益者、融資、時間點），能全部猜對的機率很低。","他的結論不是迴避，而是「校準過的謙卑」：承認不確定、搞清楚自己擅長回答哪些問題、用價格補償不確定性，並且仍願意在不確定中行動。","整篇的骨架是 11 個他自己也在琢磨的問題，以及一份可套用到任何投資的自我檢查清單。"],"qs":[{"q":"Question one: How does AI do what it does?","qzh":"問題一：AI 是怎麼做到它所做的事？","pt":"連打造 AI 的人都只能解釋它內部運作的一小部分，它解釋自己的方式也未必可靠。如果沒有人真正懂，投資人能多可靠地預測它的未來？"},{"q":"Question two: What will AI be able to do?","qzh":"問題二：AI 將能夠做到什麼？","pt":"AI 已從「歸納歷史規律」進展到某種「推理」。能否誠實、有創意、做到第二層思考，以及能力標準設在哪裡，都決定哪些工作會託付給它。"},{"q":"Question three: Who will profit?","qzh":"問題三：誰會獲利？","pt":"變革性的技術不一定讓投資人賺錢。若各家模型一樣聰明、淪為大宗商品，好處可能流向使用者；反之壟斷者則能定高價。連 AI 會不會被課稅都沒人談。"},{"q":"Question four: What will be the competitive landscape?","qzh":"問題四：競爭格局會是什麼樣子？","pt":"OpenAI 和 Anthropic 幾年前還沒人聽過，領先者也可能很快被取代。回顧 20 年前的網路業，贏家與被淘汰者都不容易事先看出來。"},{"q":"Question five: How profitable will the companies be?","qzh":"問題五：這些公司能有多高的獲利？","pt":"要估算未來盈餘得回答銷量、價格、成本、再投資與晶片折舊等問題。Marks 說 AI 比他分析過的綜合企業和網路泡沫更難預測，並提出「五年後每股盈餘在 ±50% 內」的檢驗。"},{"q":"Question six: What’s the outlook for data centers?","qzh":"問題六：資料中心的前景如何？","pt":"投資基礎設施像「賣鏟子給淘金客」，但資料中心成本大半是會過時的晶片，還有土地、電力、融資等瓶頸。它不是穩穩得分的投籃，贏家與輸家的差距可能更大。"},{"q":"Question seven: Should we be worried about our unusual reliance on this sector?","qzh":"問題七：對這個產業的異常依賴，需要擔心嗎？","pt":"AI 占經濟比重小，卻貢獻大量成長、盈餘與股市漲幅，標普 500 高度集中。過度依賴單一產業讓整體更脆弱，也讓垃圾債式的分散邏輯失效。"},{"q":"Question eight: What are the implications for the credit market?","qzh":"問題八：這對信用市場有什麼意涵？","pt":"今年 AI 相關債券發行近 6,000 億美元，占投資等級債約 12%，每年還需要數千億美元。可能推升利率、排擠其他借款人，加上循環交易、表外債務與退出條款，貸款人必須格外警覺。"},{"q":"Question nine: How predictable is AI? How answerable are these questions?","qzh":"問題九：AI 有多可預測？這些問題能得到多少解答？","pt":"Claude 替 AI 整體評 3 分（方向可預測、賺錢的部分約 2 分）。前沿實驗室只有 2 分，卻在私募市場享有溢價；有穩固租約的資料中心與電力供應商則約 5～7 分。"},{"q":"Question ten: Is AI a bubble?","qzh":"問題十：AI 是泡沫嗎？","pt":"以三個角度檢查：技術是否被炒作、支出是否過度、投資人是否付太多。Marks 認為技術本身不是幻覺，但能否獲利仍得看 AI 能不能創造足夠價值，這在現階段多半是信仰問題。"},{"q":"Question eleven: How should one think about investing in AI?","qzh":"問題十一：該如何思考投資 AI？","pt":"最具影響力也最難預測，但不確定不代表要迴避。依投資形式選擇自己能回答的問題，用夠低的價格補償不確定性，並用五個自我檢查問題審視每一筆投資。"}],"end":{"t":"結論與巴菲特 1979 年名言","pt":"Marks 提醒：有兩種投資人會賠很多錢，一種什麼都不懂，另一種什麼都懂。對 AI 而言，最重要的是接受自己不是無所不知。最後他引用巴菲特 1979 年的話：「未來從來都不明朗；你在股市裡要為一致樂觀的共識付出很高的價格；不確定性其實是長期價值買家的朋友。」"}},"blocks":[{"kind":"p","text":"**Should we invest in artificial intelligence, and can we do so intelligently? **That’s what I’m going to address in this memo."},{"kind":"p","text":"On one hand, I’m not a tech person, and I don’t claim to have above-average knowledge of artificial intelligence (or technology investing, for that matter). On the other, AI is the biggest development of our time and could be one of the most impactful things the world has ever seen, so investors don’t have the option to abstain from thinking about it."},{"kind":"p","text":"With that disclaimer, I’m going to share my deliberations, put forth the questions I’m wrestling with (not all of which are directly relevant to investing in AI), and provide my thinking about them. While my limited knowledge prevents me from definitively answering these questions, I hope to provide a service by assembling them in one place. And while news on AI comes hot and heavy these days, with security prices for AI and AI-adjacent companies gyrating up and down, I’m not going to try to keep up, since what I have to say relates to the long-term outlook for AI, not what security prices will be tomorrow."},{"kind":"p","text":"Investing consists of just one thing: positioning capital to benefit from future events, and most investors cope by relying on forecasts. But not everything is equally predictable, and not all forecasters are equally skilled. Thus, to invest in AI based on expectations about the future – and I think that’s all we have – we must consider both what we think the future may hold and the chance we can get it right."},{"kind":"p","text":"I’ve written in the past about the fact that people who engage in top-down investing have to be right about the outlook for the economy, the industry, the company, and the market. If they have a 60% probability of getting each one right – which is probably generous – what’s the probability of getting all four right? The answer is 13% (0.64), assuming the outcomes are independent of each other."},{"kind":"p","text":"**In the case of AI, there are an unusually large number of important variables, each of which complicates the decision of how to invest.** An investor in AI has to be at least somewhat right about the evolution of the technology, the demand for compute, the competitive structure, the beneficiaries of the technology, the availability of financing, and the timing of all the above. Assessing these key elements seems to me to be unusually challenging. In that case, what’s the combined probability that any thesis is correct? That’s just something to think about as you read."},{"kind":"p","text":"**Lastly, bear in mind that (a) we can never know everything about an investment, (b) the biggest mistakes are made when people think they do, and (c) the incompleteness of our knowledge regarding the future can’t be permitted to completely prevent us from taking action.** It’s the investor’s job to bear risk in the pursuit of profit. How much and in what form are decisions we each must make. But given its vast potential, we can’t decline to participate in AI just because the future is unclear."},{"kind":"h","text":"Question one: How does AI do what it does?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Before getting more granular, I’m going to start with a big-picture, non-financial question that I find particularly interesting. Since I have absolutely no knowledge on this subject, I went to Anthropic’s Claude for some answers. Here’s some of what it said:"},{"kind":"p","text":"We know what AI is made of and how it was built almost perfectly, but we know surprisingly little about how it actually does what it does. . . ."},{"kind":"p","text":"We also know the training recipe: show it enormous amounts of text, have it guess the next word, and nudge billions of internal settings slightly each time it guesses wrong. Repeat that trillions of times. . . ."},{"kind":"p","text":"Nobody wrote the rules for how it answers the question. Those rules emerged from the nudging, spread across billions of numbers that mean nothing individually. The best phrase I know for this is that these systems are grown rather than designed. Researchers in “interpretability” are making real progress. They have found, for example, that a model writing a rhyming poem picks the rhyme word before it writes the line, which means it plans ahead. But they can explain only small slices of what goes on. It’s like having a complete record of every trade in the market and still not knowing why prices moved. . . ."},{"kind":"p","text":"When I explain how I reached an answer, research suggests my explanation doesn’t always match what actually happened inside. A model asked how it did a sum may describe the schoolbook method while internally doing something very different. So the honest summary is that we’ve built a mind-like thing we can inspect completely and still can’t fully explain. And when it explains itself, it may be no more reliable than we are when we explain ourselves [interestingly, here Claude talks like it’s a person]. . . ."},{"kind":"p","text":"As you think about answering the questions that follow below, consider these: Does anyone truly understand how AI works? How will AI evolve and what will it be able to do and when? How will users put it to work? What impact will it have on users’ profitability? Investors may not have to know all these things, but it’s my understanding that few people have significant insight into these matters. If that’s the case, can anyone reliably answer the questions that follow?"},{"kind":"h","text":"Question two: What will AI be able to do?"},{"kind":"p","text":"What AI is great at is evolving. Just a few years ago, what AI could do was generally summed up as knowing everything that has occurred before, identifying historical patterns, extrapolating them, and using this ability to perform tasks. This has already made huge changes in some areas, like coding, and promises to alter many aspects of life as we presently know it. But now, AI has gone well beyond that, to something that probably can fairly be described as “reasoning.”"},{"kind":"p","text":"Just this one area poses several questions that are both stimulating and daunting. AI’s full potential will be subject to the answers to some of these:"},{"kind":"p","text":"Can AI’s process be made to reflect our preferences, values, and goals, or will it apply its own?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Can AI be depended on to be honest, humble, ethical, and loyal? Will it be accurate and objective or subject to influence and manipulation? Will it admit when it’s wrong or doesn’t know an answer? Can it give us skepticism rather than sycophancy? Will it become a threat to humans and seek to advance its own interests instead of ours?"},{"kind":"p","text":"How will it do in areas such as creativity, originality, aesthetics, subjectivity, and intuition? Will it be able to perform what I call “second-level thinking” – thinking in a way that’s different from that of most people and at a higher level – leading to superior solutions?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Will it be able to think about the future and “blue sky” to the same degree as humans?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Can it be instructed to furnish primary sources (as opposed to published citations) and provide counterarguments?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Will one model be able to do it all, or will different models be best at different things? If so, how will we know which one to use?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Will artificial general intelligence – the ability to do everything a human can do – be the standard? Will AGI mean the ability to do the same things as (a) the average person, (b) a person in the 90th percentile, or (c) the smartest person in the world? In other words, will there be varying levels of knowledge and things AI doesn’t know?"},{"kind":"p","text":"All these things bear on what work will be entrusted to AI."},{"kind":"h","text":"Question three: Who will profit?"},{"kind":"p","text":"As Warren Buffett pointed out about the internet during the tech bubble, transformative technologies don’t necessarily reward investors. We know AI will increase efficiency, but will it increase profitability? And whose? If several AI models can become equally smart on a given subject, will their service become an undifferentiated commodity that sells on the basis of price? If it does, will profitability and ROI be ground down, in which case the economic benefits may go more to the users of AI than to the vendors? What does all this imply for the return on the trillions of dollars being invested in AI infrastructure?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Or, on the other hand, will some AI companies acquire capabilities that make them monopolies whose products are essential for companies’ success and even survival? If so, think about the prices they’ll be able to command. (The big chip producers – Nvidia and TSMC – are racking up the big profits these days, demonstrating the value of a strong competitive position.)"},{"kind":"p","text":"Lastly on this subject, I haven’t heard much about whether and how AI will be taxed. Given that one of AI’s most likely effects will be replacing workers, and given governments’ need to replace the tax revenues that previously came from taxing the people AI puts out of work, **what’s to say AI won’t be taxed like people? **Many of today’s top earners are taxed at combined rates of 50% or more. What tax bracket will AI be in?"},{"kind":"h","text":"Question four: What will be the competitive landscape?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Can we know which companies will succeed? With what degree of certainty? How strong are the moats surrounding today’s leaders?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Perhaps the most startling characteristic of AI is the speed at which it makes progress. **In that connection, I’m struck by the fact that today’s leading model labs, OpenAI and Anthropic, were unknown to most of us just a few years ago. If companies can forge ahead that fast in the world of AI, can they likewise be supplanted by others, or at least challenged? **If it’s possible, consider the vast resources and potential of the so-called “hyperscalers”: Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet, Meta, and Oracle. Who will win among these incumbents? Or will it be companies we’ve never heard of? How about companies that don’t exist yet?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Think about the probability 20-25 years ago that the leaders then would still be the leaders now. Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, and Google came through that era as winners, but Napster, Yahoo, and Netscape fell by the wayside. It takes a lot to maintain a lead in a rapidly changing technology. Add in AI’s novelty and speed of progress and ask, “what’s the probability today’s AI leaders will still be the leaders in five or ten years?” **If the competitive picture can shift as I suggest, how much is a stake in today’s leaders worth?**"},{"kind":"h","text":"Question five: How profitable will the companies be?"},{"kind":"p","text":"The value investor estimates a company’s earnings or cash flows in the future, discounts them back to the present, and compares the result with today’s stock price. To predict earnings in the future, we need answers to questions like these:"},{"kind":"p","text":"How many units of its product will it sell?"},{"kind":"p","text":"What will be the selling price per unit?"},{"kind":"p","text":"What will each unit cost to produce?"},{"kind":"p","text":"How much of the profit will need to be reinvested in developing the next product?"},{"kind":"p","text":"What will be the company’s resulting return on investment?"},{"kind":"p","text":"While answering these questions for any company requires dealing with uncertainty, doing so in connection with AI introduces particularly novel considerations like these:"},{"kind":"p","text":"What will its product be capable of doing, and how will customers put it to work?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Will the product be priced as a commodity or a unique, essential good?"},{"kind":"p","text":"How long will chips last, and how fast will data-center depreciation have to be charged off?"},{"kind":"p","text":"For how long will today’s breakneck spending race continue?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Will enough money be made to produce a decent return on today’s capital expenditures?"},{"kind":"p","text":"I could certainly be wrong, but I don’t remember puzzling over uncertainties of this magnitude in my days analyzing the conglomerates in the 1970s, or in connection with the internet and e-commerce in 1999-2000. Those businesses, as novel and complex as they were, seem simple and straightforward compared to AI."},{"kind":"p","text":"So, I wonder: can we project 2030 earnings for today’s hyperscalers and leading frontier labs (let alone companies that mostly consist of conjecture)? Do we have to be able to predict earnings reasonably accurately to invest? If we come up with estimates, how much confidence should we have in them? And if we can’t, how can we think about investing in AI companies?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Here’s the test I propose nowadays: Tell me what you think the e.p.s. for a given AI company will be in five years. **Will you bet that the eventual answer will be within +/- 50% of your figure? **(E.g., if you guess $10 per share, how confident are you that the actual figure will be between $5 and $15?)"},{"kind":"h","text":"Question six: What’s the outlook for data centers?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Historically, data centers were digital warehouses that stored data and hosted computer applications and websites. The term data center (or “AI factory”) has evolved to refer to the workplaces of AI. Analogous to auto plants, they’re where AI is produced, ranging from storing information and training models to providing time on the system (called “compute”). Data centers are a critical element in the development of the AI industry. The construction of data centers, and thus the performing of compute, is subject to constraints – physical and otherwise."},{"kind":"p","text":"While the future of AI is exciting, some people might prefer to take a cautious approach to participating in it. This suggests investment via tangible infrastructure rather than developers and vendors of the emerging technology, a strategy akin to selling picks and shovels to gold miners rather than trying to figure out which miners will find gold. In theory, this makes determining the ultimate winners less critical since whoever ends up winning would still need to use the infrastructure. Compute is in short supply today, adding to the attractiveness of data centers for investment. However, a number of factors must be considered:"},{"kind":"p","text":"When we think about “infrastructure,” the phrase “bricks and mortar” often comes to mind, and it’s that solidity that can make data centers appear the safer AI option. But a relatively small part of the cost of a data center goes for the building itself – the “shell.” Chips or “GPUs” make up most of the cost, and that (and the risk of obsolescence) is generally borne by the center user."},{"kind":"p","text":"Large building sites will have to be located and purchased, and data-center developers will have to overcome regulatory hurdles and resistance from citizens opposed to data centers in their backyards."},{"kind":"p","text":"Connectivity to users of inference via available fiber will be a necessity."},{"kind":"p","text":"Data-center builders will have to deal with potential bottlenecks in terms of the needed electric power, cooling equipment, and construction services."},{"kind":"p","text":"Limitations on the availability of financing for data centers could constrain the pace at which they’re constructed, and the cost of financing for future projects could surprise."},{"kind":"p","text":"Interestingly, the potential bottlenecks mentioned above could complicate life for would-be builders of data centers and limit their growth. On the flip side, any constriction of the growth in the supply of “compute” – as implied by the success last week of a number of moratoriums on data center construction – should make the ones that presently exist or that can be built in the future more profitable and thus more valuable."},{"kind":"p","text":"The key here is that data centers aren’t a “layup.” The intelligent investor has to find opportunities that are insulated from the uncertainties listed above and won’t be subject to overbuilding and thus oversupply of compute. As in all forms of investing, there’ll be successful data-center investors and less-successful ones. Given the dynamics of AI, however, I think the successes of the winners and failures of the losers may be even more pronounced than is usual in “infrastructure.”"},{"kind":"h","text":"Question seven: Should we be worried about our unusual reliance on this sector?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Today, AI is responsible for a disproportionate share of what’s going on in the investment environment, introducing considerable uncertainty for all investors (and all participants in the economy), not just for investors in AI:"},{"kind":"p","text":"AI represents a small part of the U.S. economy but is responsible for an outsized part of economic growth."},{"kind":"p","text":"AI’s contribution to GDP growth currently comes overwhelmingly through capital expenditures, primarily for construction of data centers. These are rising rapidly while the rest of construction is in decline."},{"kind":"p","text":"For 14 consecutive quarters through June 30 of this year, earnings grew faster for the “Magnificent 7” – mostly the hyperscalers – than for the rest of the S&P 500."},{"kind":"p","text":"Some AI companies have been generating large gains from equity investments in fellow AI-related companies, making them top contributors to S&P 500 earnings. FactSet reported that in the second quarter, Alphabet and Amazon notched a total of $151 billion of gains on equity investments, mostly on Anthropic and SpaceX (which now includes xAI)."},{"kind":"p","text":"AI’s impact on aggregate earnings doesn’t stop with the sector. Sellers of associated components are reporting strong earnings as well, and there are knock-on effects as AI workers and AI-adjacent workers spend their pay on rent, food, cars, and other things far removed from AI itself."},{"kind":"p","text":"AI has disproportionately contributed to stock market gains, with a group of 42 AI-related companies generating around two-thirds of the S&P 500’s price gains since January 2024, according to J.P. Morgan."},{"kind":"p","text":"The sum of these trends has left the S&P 500 highly concentrated in a few AI and tech companies."},{"kind":"p","text":"The key observation is that the factors our economy and markets depend on today are relatively undiversified. Exposure to AI constitutes an unusually powerful “fault line” that runs through portfolios and has the potential to dominate all aspects of the investment environment. Negative developments with regard to chip life, corporate strategy, or demand for compute could have far-reaching consequences. **It’s incontrovertible that disproportionate reliance on a single sector makes the economy and stock market more vulnerable than they would be if the factors underlying them were broad-based.**"},{"kind":"p","text":"When Michael Milken pioneered the issuance of sub-investment grade debt in 1977-78, he ushered in an investment-industry-wide willingness to accept risk as long as it’s viewed as being accompanied by enough potential return to compensate for the risk. His thesis rested on two cornerstones: (a) incremental yield that potentially compensates for the losses on the bonds that default and (b) **enough diversification so that a few defaults won’t spoil the overall portfolio outcome. **But today, our stock and debt markets are heavily reliant on a single factor: the success of AI."},{"kind":"h","text":"Question eight: What are the implications for the credit market?"},{"kind":"p","text":"AI-related debt issuance has totaled nearly $600 billion globally so far this year, according to Goldman Sachs, comprising debt issued by the hyperscalers, in data-center/project financings, and by other AI-linked borrowers. Around $400 billion of this went to the U.S. investment grade debt market, representing over one-fifth of total IG supply and taking the share of AI-related bonds in the Bloomberg USD IG Corporate index to around 12%. For the U.S. high yield bond market – into which the hyperscalers don’t directly issue – the weighting is more like 6%. Clearly, AI-related debt issuance is one of this year’s major capital-market developments."},{"kind":"p","text":"Going forward, AI seems likely to require an average of $1 trillion of new capital per year in the period ahead, and hundreds of billions of that are likely to continue to be raised each year through the issuance of debt. What will be the impact on interest rates of AI’s demand for debt capital?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Since expanding capacity and developing new products are imperatives for the hyperscalers and frontier labs – and since they don’t want to see competition crop up – these companies likely can’t afford to slow their investment in AI just because interest rates may be higher than they expected. That means they have to be “takers” of financing, even if it is in short supply and increases in cost."},{"kind":"p","text":"In the past, economists used to talk about “crowding out,” in which aggressive government borrowing makes it hard for the private sector to find the debt capital it needs. At present, it feels to me that the demand for debt from rate-insensitive hyperscalers willing to pay whatever it takes has the potential to elevate the interest rates the government must pay. Hasn’t that contributed to the rate rise we’ve seen to date?"},{"kind":"p","text":"In the same vein, will the equity markets be capable of providing the hundreds of billions of new equity investment required each year, supplementing the companies’ cash flow? The amount raised in traditional U.S. IPOs was below $50 billion in eight of the last ten years (the exceptions being almost $80 billion in 2020 and $140 billion in the boom year of 2021). I suspect there’s just so much new capital available in a given year; won’t AI’s appetites for both debt and equity ultimately compete against each other for it?"},{"kind":"p","text":"In the past, the highly profitable non-AI businesses of the hyperscalers produced massive free cash flow that rendered them debt-free, cash-rich, and highly rated. Things are different now, and these companies are spending their cash hoards and current cash flow, and they’re borrowing more. Their strong investment-grade ratings enable them to easily finance their expansion in AI. But as this process wears on, might their creditworthiness and/or ratings change? What are the possible implications for those who lend against the high ratings today and watch as the companies increase their leverage?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Some of the data-center financing taking place is facilitated by the investment-grade ratings of the hyperscaler tenants. Other debt is collateralized by chips. But investors have to be very conscious of “out clauses” in some data-center leases that can enable tenants or guarantors to terminate their obligation under certain conditions – such as the failure of the center to operate at 99.9% reliability – and of the potential risk that collateral in the form of chips won’t last as long as the debt."},{"kind":"p","text":"What are the implications of the industry’s so-called “circular transactions,” in which companies finance purchases of their products? We see frequent instances of lending and related buying between chipmakers, hyperscalers, frontier labs, and data-center builders. Sometimes money goes from A to B and back to A. Sometimes it goes from A to B, and then to financing for C, which spends it with A. Are these transactions the parties would enter into on an arm’s length basis, or are vendors subsidizing buyers to support demand and protect market share for their products? What are the risks associated with this interdependence? And what is the impact on the hyperscalers’ creditworthiness of borrowing on the part of special-purpose vehicles that keep debt off balance sheets – is the parent behind the debt or isn’t it?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Finally, what are the effects of borrowing in a time of exuberance surrounding an exciting technological advance? In the end, we have the confluence of several factors:"},{"kind":"p","text":"a massive desire to borrow on the part of AI companies,"},{"kind":"p","text":"investor excitement surrounding AI, and thus a willingness to lend,"},{"kind":"p","text":"heavy concentration on AI in the capital markets, as described above, and"},{"kind":"p","text":"so-called “circular transactions” between participants in the AI industry."},{"kind":"p","text":"Lenders to AI face a new era, marked by debt concentrated in a single industry, rapid change in industry fundamentals, and a high degree of reliance on the level of future revenues from sales of compute. **Lenders will have to be exceptionally vigilant regarding the creditworthiness of the borrowers they back and the structure/terms through which they do it.**"},{"kind":"h","text":"Question nine: How predictable is AI? How answerable are these questions?"},{"kind":"p","text":"In memos and in my book Mastering the Market Cycle: Getting the Odds on Your Side, I’ve written that for someone attempting to deal with the future, a prediction isn’t enough. In addition to your prediction, you need a view on the probability your prediction is correct. It’s my view that with regard to AI – given its technical complexity, unprecedented nature, and high rate of change – that probability is unusually low."},{"kind":"p","text":"I wanted to quantify AI’s predictability, but I worried that doing so was well beyond my competence. Thus, I asked Claude to help by ranking AI on a scale from 1 (unknowable) to 10 (fully predictable). Its answers were highly informative. Claude summed up on the subject, reflecting some of the uncertainties discussed above (emphasis added):"},{"kind":"p","text":"**My overall judgment: AI as a whole scores about a 3. **That number hides a split. The direction is fairly predictable, maybe a six or seven: the technology will keep improving and spread through the economy. The things investors get paid for are much less predictable, around a 2: how fast it happens, how big the profits are, and who keeps them."},{"kind":"p","text":"I also asked Claude to rate subsectors of the AI industry on predictability and identify parts of the non-AI economy it considers comparable in that regard. Here are what it describes as its “judgment calls”:"},{"kind":"table","head":["AI 子產業","可預測性評分","可預測性相當的傳統產業（分數）"],"headEn":["AI Sector","Predictability Score","Comparable Industry"],"rows":[["Regulated power utilities serving AI load","6-7","Regulated utilities (6)"],["Data centers leased long-term to big tech companies","5-6","Large industrials (5-6)"],["Hyperscalers","5","N/A"],["Chips","4","Airlines, shipping (3-4)"],["Speculative or “neocloud” data centers","2-3","Telecom/fiber ’98-’01 (2-3)"],["Frontier labs (OpenAI, Anthropic, xAI, etc.)","2","—"],["Early-stage VC","1-2","—"]]},{"kind":"p","text":"For me, the surprise was the frontier labs’ score of 2, which I take to mean “practically unknowable.” These seem to me to be impressive companies with big leads, but Claude notes as follows: “These businesses are close to binary. Capital needs are huge, prices per unit of capability keep falling fast, and it’s unclear whether a lasting moat exists.” (Ironic, isn’t it, given that Claude belongs to Anthropic?) Intriguingly, these most uncertain propositions according to Claude seem to be priced at a premium in the private market. It’ll be interesting to see how the shares of OpenAI and Anthropic, both of which have filed for IPOs, are priced publicly and how they trade afterwards. And note that much of the equity gains that goosed S&P 500 earnings in the second quarter – described on page 6 – were related to this sector, which is described as “least predictable”; how lasting will they prove to be?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Finally on the subject of predictability, I’ll take this opportunity for a word about geopolitics, which with regard to AI generally means “China.” Nearly all the leading-edge chips come from Taiwan’s TSMC. The U.S. restricts sales to China of the most advanced chips. This encourages China to offer low-cost models that can undercut sales of more powerful U.S. models. What happens to the U.S.’s competitive position if this changes? Will chips from China catch up with or pass today’s leaders? Will China ever be in position to discourage or even prevent sales of Taiwanese chips to the U.S.? And, will worries about China’s intentions blunt efforts to impose safety guardrails on the AI industry?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Clearly, there’s a lot investors don’t know and can’t predict about AI. **The question is how much risk will you take and in what form? There are many parts to the AI industry, and (according to Claude) they range from the frontier labs, with regard to which we’re largely in the dark, to securely leased data centers and their power providers, with predictability roughly comparable to that of large industrials and regulated utilities. **"},{"kind":"h","text":"Question ten: Is AI a bubble?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Worry over some of the questions discussed above has begun to surface in recent weeks. A confluence of negatives in the investment environment arose, centering around inflation, the federal deficits and debt, the resulting increase in interest rates, and the seeming intractability of the war in Iran. To these general issues were added some considerations specific to AI, such as:"},{"kind":"p","text":"competition from new models; cheaper models with fewer bells and whistles; low-priced Chinese models; and open-source models that are free to download,"},{"kind":"p","text":"declining prices per “token” (the unit in which model usage is priced),"},{"kind":"p","text":"the possibility of slowing growth in the use of compute,"},{"kind":"p","text":"reported sales growth that falls short of expectations, and"},{"kind":"p","text":"concern about some companies’ high leverage levels and heavy dependence on future financing."},{"kind":"p","text":"Questions that are easily dismissed in good times can worry investors when their equanimity is shaken. And then we find, as I say, that whereas in real life things fluctuate between pretty good and not so hot, in the minds of investors they go from flawless to hopeless. The things AI investors are worrying about these days are nothing new, but a changed investment climate has rendered them sufficient to darken the mood."},{"kind":"p","text":"**Even though the subject has been debated for well over a year – including by me – people keep asking: is it a bubble? **First, we need a definition of a bubble, and I want to supply mine: irrational exuberance leading to imprudent behavior, excessive investment, and too-high prices for participation. I think an AI bubble, like any possible bubble surrounding a technological advance, has to be considered in at least three ways. **Is the technology itself overhyped and likely to disappoint? Are the amounts companies are spending excessive and fated to produce inadequate returns? And are investors taking too much risk and paying too much for stakes in this field? **Reaching conclusions on these subjects relies heavily on the answers to the above questions. I don’t feel much has changed in these regards since I last wrote a memo on AI in February:"},{"kind":"p","text":"I still think AI itself will prove powerful and consequential and change corporations, the economy, and life speaking broadly in ways we can’t even imagine. So no, the technology isn’t an illusion."},{"kind":"p","text":"A great deal of discussion is taking place regarding the revenues that will have to be generated in the future to justify the capital expenditures. But until we know something about how AI will be used and who will profit, we can’t assess the wisdom of the companies’ spending."},{"kind":"p","text":"And until knowledge of these things enables us to reasonably estimate the companies’ earnings and the profitability of the infrastructure, we can’t judge the appropriateness of the prices being paid for stocks or the yields being offered on debt."},{"kind":"p","text":"While there are a lot of questions swirling around AI – some of which I’ve described above – the key boils down to this rather complex one: **will AI provide sufficient value to users to warrant sales of compute and prices for compute high enough to result in adequate returns on the trillions of dollars being invested in it (and for which companies)? **"},{"kind":"p","text":"The conclusion I draw from my conversations with people I consider knowledgeable is that, at this point, answering this question is largely a matter of faith. That reminds me of a quote attributed to American economist and writer Stuart Chase:"},{"kind":"p","text":"For those who believe, no proof is necessary. For those who don’t believe, no proof is possible."},{"kind":"p","text":"Try as I may, I can’t come up with a more appropriate resolution to the questions surrounding AI than this."},{"kind":"h","text":"Question eleven: How should one think about investing in AI?"},{"kind":"p","text":"I consider artificial intelligence the most consequential and least predictable development for investors in at least a generation. **But investors needn’t dismiss something just because it’s uncertain. **The right response isn’t paralysis but (a) calibrated humility regarding strategy selection, position sizing, and risk-taking and (b) a willingness to act in the presence of uncertainty."},{"kind":"p","text":"Different aspects of investing in AI require answering different questions. An equity investor in a frontier lab may need to be right about technological progress, competitive positioning, pricing power, future profitability, and valuation. A lender financing a data center under a long-term contract may need to get fewer things right, but the things that matter – the tenant’s creditworthiness, contract terms, power availability, and residual asset value – are quite different. Thus, a key question is, “which of the questions am I or my organization well suited to answer, and which uncertainties am I willing to bear in the absence of that expertise?”"},{"kind":"p","text":"Everything else being equal, a high level of uncertainty can be acceptable to investors if the price of the investment is low enough relative to its intrinsic value. At least in theory, the sectors rated lower on Claude’s predictability scale can be the better investment prospects if they’re sufficiently cheaper than the more predictable ones. At the end of the day, intelligent investing is largely about price. **Note, however, that in the case of AI, thinking about intrinsic value and thus cheapness is greatly complicated by the questions raised above. **"},{"kind":"p","text":"In an effort to tie this all together, I’ll leave you with a list of questions that might help you reach your decisions. Note, however, that these are questions that apply to all investments, not just AI:"},{"kind":"p","text":"How many things have to go right for me to earn an acceptable return?"},{"kind":"p","text":"If my central assumptions prove wrong, how much could I lose?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Which risks am I being compensated for, and which am I simply assuming?"},{"kind":"p","text":"How much optimism is already incorporated in the price?"},{"kind":"p","text":"How does this investment change the overall risk profile of my portfolio? Does it amplify the risk I’m already carrying, or does it diversify it?"},{"kind":"p","text":"There’s an old saying, and it seems highly applicable to AI: “Two kinds of investors lose a lot of money: those who know nothing and those who know everything.” I think in the case of AI, it’s unusually important to accept that you don’t know everything. AI is sure to produce a lot of surprises, but they needn’t be unbearable. The thing to ponder is, what’s the probability an AI investment will disappoint . . . and what’s the probability it won’t?"},{"kind":"p","text":"I’ll conclude with a great quote I happened upon the other day:"},{"kind":"p","text":"**[The] argument is made that there are just too many question marks about the near future: wouldn’t it be better to wait until things clear up a bit? **You know the prose: “Maintain buying reserves until current uncertainties are resolved, etc.” Before reaching for that crutch, face up to two unpleasant facts: The future is never clear; you pay a very high price in the stock market for a cheery consensus. Uncertainty actually is the friend of the buyer of long-term values. (Emphasis added)"},{"kind":"p","text":"The perfect ending for a memo on AI, isn’t it?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Note, however, that it came from one of my oldest files: timeless wisdom from Warren Buffett that appeared in Forbes magazine on August 6, 1979. **I’m always happy to give Warren the last word.**"}],"zh":["**我們該不該投資人工智慧？又能不能投得聰明？**這就是本篇備忘錄要談的事。","一方面，我不是科技人，也不敢說自己對人工智慧（或科技投資）的了解高於平均。另一方面，AI 是我們這個時代最重大的發展，可能是世界有史以來影響最深遠的事物之一，所以投資人沒有「不去想它」這個選項。","先把話說在前面，接下來我要分享自己的思考，提出我一直在琢磨的問題（並非每一題都直接和投資 AI 有關），再說明我的想法。我的知識有限，沒辦法替這些問題下定論，但把它們集中整理在一處，或許也算有點貢獻。這陣子 AI 的消息滿天飛，AI 與相關公司的股價漲跌劇烈，不過我不打算追著新聞跑，因為我要談的是 AI 的長期前景，而不是明天的股價。","投資只有一件事：把資金部署在能從未來事件中受益的位置，而多數投資人靠預測來應付這件事。但並非所有事情都一樣可預測，也不是每位預測者都一樣高明。因此，要根據對未來的預期來投資 AI（我認為我們手上也只有這個），就得同時考慮兩件事：我們認為未來會怎麼發展，以及我們猜對的機率有多高。","我以前寫過，採取由上而下投資法的人，必須同時看對經濟、產業、公司和市場的前景。假設每一項猜對的機率是 60%（這已經算寬鬆了），四項全部猜對的機率是多少？答案是 13%（0.6 的四次方），前提是各項結果彼此獨立。","**以 AI 來說，重要變數的數量異常龐大，每一個都讓投資決策更複雜。**投資 AI 的人，至少得大致看對技術的演進、對算力的需求、競爭結構、誰是這項技術的受益者、資金能不能籌到，以及上述每一件事的時間點。我覺得要評估這些關鍵要素格外困難。這樣一來，任何一套論點同時成立的機率有多高？讀下去的時候，請把這個問題放在心裡。","**最後請記住：（a）我們永遠不可能完全了解一項投資；（b）最大的錯誤往往發生在人們自以為完全了解的時候；（c）我們對未來的認知不完整，但不能因此完全不行動。**承擔風險以追求獲利，正是投資人的工作。要承擔多少、用什麼形式承擔，是每個人都得自己決定的事。可是 AI 的潛力如此巨大，我們不能只因為未來不明朗就拒絕參與。","問題一：AI 是怎麼做到它所做的事？","在進入更細的部分之前，我想先從一個宏觀、與金錢無關、但我覺得特別有趣的問題開始。因為我對這個主題完全沒有知識，所以我去請教 Anthropic 的 Claude。以下是它回答的部分內容：","我們對 AI 是由什麼組成、又是怎麼建造出來的，幾乎一清二楚；但對它究竟是怎麼做到這些事的，知道的卻少得驚人……","我們也知道訓練的配方：給它看海量的文字，讓它猜下一個字，每次猜錯，就把內部數十億個設定稍微調整一點。這樣重複幾兆次……","沒有人寫下它回答問題的規則。這些規則是從一次次調整中自己長出來的，分散在數十億個單獨看毫無意義的數字裡。我所知道最貼切的說法是：這些系統是「養成」的，不是「設計」的。研究「可解釋性」的人正取得實質進展，例如他們發現，模型在寫押韻的詩時，會在寫那一行之前先選好押韻的字，也就是說它會預先規劃。但他們只能解釋其中一小部分。這就像手上握有市場上每一筆交易的完整紀錄，卻仍然不知道價格為什麼會動……","當我解釋自己是怎麼得出答案時，研究顯示我的解釋不一定符合內部實際發生的事。一個模型被問到怎麼算一道加法，可能會描述課本上的算法，內部做的卻是非常不同的東西。所以老實的總結是：我們造出了一個像心智的東西，可以徹底檢視它，卻仍無法完全解釋它。而當它解釋自己時，可靠程度可能不比我們人類解釋自己時高［有意思的是，這裡 Claude 說話的口氣像個人］……","在思考下面各個問題時，請想想這幾件事：真的有人了解 AI 是怎麼運作的嗎？AI 會如何演進、能做什麼、什麼時候能做到？使用者會怎麼運用它？它會對使用者的獲利能力造成什麼影響？投資人未必需要弄懂這些事，但就我所知，對這些議題有深入洞見的人很少。如果真是如此，有誰能可靠地回答接下來的問題？","問題二：AI 將能夠做到什麼？","AI 最擅長的事就是進化。就在幾年前，AI 能做的事大致可以歸納為：掌握過去發生過的一切、找出歷史規律、把規律往外推，再用這種能力去執行任務。這已經在某些領域（例如寫程式）帶來巨大改變，也有望改變我們目前所知的許多生活層面。但現在，AI 已經遠遠超過這個階段，到了或許可以公允地稱為「推理」的程度。","光是這一個領域，就帶出幾個既刺激又令人卻步的問題。AI 的潛力能發揮到什麼程度，要看其中一些問題的答案：","AI 的運作過程能不能被設計成反映我們的偏好、價值觀和目標，還是它會套用自己的？","我們能不能指望 AI 誠實、謙虛、有道德、忠誠？它會準確又客觀，還是容易受到影響和操弄？它會承認自己錯了或不知道答案嗎？它能給我們懷疑的眼光，而不是一味奉承嗎？它會不會變成對人類的威脅，並且追求自己的利益而非我們的利益？","在創意、原創性、美感、主觀判斷和直覺這些方面，它會表現得如何？它能不能做到我所說的「第二層思考」，也就是用和多數人不同、層次更高的方式思考，進而得出更好的解決方案？","它能不能像人類一樣思考未來、天馬行空地想像？","能不能指示它提供第一手資料來源（而不是別人發表過的引用），並提出反面論點？","會不會有一個模型什麼都能做，還是不同模型各有擅長的領域？若是後者，我們要怎麼知道該用哪一個？","通用人工智慧，也就是能做到人類所能做的一切，會成為標準嗎？AGI 指的是做到和（a）一般人、（b）前 10% 的人，還是（c）全世界最聰明的人一樣的事？換句話說，AI 的知識會不會有高低層次之分，也有它不知道的事？","這些事情都關係到哪些工作會被託付給 AI。","問題三：誰會獲利？","正如巴菲特在科技泡沫時期談到網際網路所指出的，具變革性的技術未必能讓投資人受益。我們知道 AI 會提高效率，但它會提高獲利嗎？又是誰的獲利？如果好幾個 AI 模型在某個領域都變得一樣聰明，它們的服務會不會變成沒有差異的大宗商品，只能靠價格競爭？若是如此，獲利與投資報酬率會不會被一路壓低，使經濟利益更多流向 AI 的使用者，而不是供應商？這對目前投入 AI 基礎建設的數兆美元，又意味著什麼樣的報酬？","反過來說，會不會有些 AI 公司取得了足以壟斷的能力，產品成為企業成功甚至生存所必需？若是如此，想想它們能開出多高的價格。（大型晶片廠 Nvidia 和台積電這陣子賺進豐厚利潤，正說明了穩固競爭地位的價值。）","關於這個主題，最後一點是，我還沒聽到太多有關 AI 是否會被課稅、以及如何課稅的討論。AI 最可能產生的效應之一是取代勞工，而政府需要補回原本向這些失業者課稅得來的稅收，**那誰能保證 AI 不會像人一樣被課稅？**現在許多高所得者的綜合稅率達 50% 以上。AI 又會落在哪個稅率級距？","問題四：競爭格局會是什麼樣子？","我們能知道哪些公司會成功嗎？有多大把握？今天這些領導者身邊的護城河有多深？","AI 最令人吃驚的特徵，或許是它的進步速度。**就這一點而言，讓我印象深刻的是，如今領先的模型實驗室 OpenAI 和 Anthropic，幾年前對我們大多數人還很陌生。如果公司在 AI 世界裡能那麼快地衝到前面，那麼它們同樣可能被別人取代，或至少受到挑戰嗎？**若有可能，想想所謂「超大規模雲端業者」亞馬遜、微軟、Alphabet、Meta 和甲骨文的龐大資源與潛力。這些現有業者之中誰會勝出？還是會是我們從沒聽過的公司？那些現在還不存在的公司呢？","想想 20 到 25 年前，當時的領導者到今天仍是領導者的機率有多高。微軟、亞馬遜、蘋果和 Google 在那個年代存活下來成為贏家，但 Napster、雅虎和 Netscape 卻被淘汰了。在快速變動的技術領域要維持領先，需要很大的功夫。再加上 AI 的新穎和進步速度，不妨問問：「今天的 AI 領導者在五年或十年後還是領導者的機率有多高？」**如果競爭局勢真如我所說可能改變，那麼持有今天領導者的股份值多少錢？**","問題五：這些公司能有多高的獲利？","價值投資人會估算一家公司未來的盈餘或現金流，把它折現回現在，再和今天的股價比較。要預測未來的盈餘，我們需要回答下面這類問題：","它的產品能賣出多少單位？","每單位的售價會是多少？","每單位的生產成本是多少？","有多少利潤必須再投入，以開發下一代產品？","最後的投資報酬率會是多少？","對任何公司回答這些問題都得面對不確定性，而就 AI 來說，還會碰上一些格外新穎的考量，例如：","它的產品能做到什麼？客戶會怎麼運用它？","產品的定價會是大宗商品式的，還是獨一無二、不可或缺的商品？","晶片能用多久？資料中心的折舊必須多快攤提完畢？","今天這場狂飆式的燒錢競賽還會持續多久？","賺到的錢夠不夠，能對今天的資本支出帶來像樣的報酬？","我可能說錯，但我不記得自己在 1970 年代分析綜合企業集團時，或在 1999 至 2000 年研究網際網路與電子商務時，曾為這麼大規模的不確定性傷腦筋。那些事業雖然新穎又複雜，和 AI 比起來卻顯得單純直接。","所以我不禁想問：我們能預測今天的超大規模雲端業者和領先的前沿實驗室在 2030 年的盈餘嗎（更別說那些大半只是臆測的公司）？投資是不是一定要能相當準確地預測盈餘？就算我們做出了估計，該對它有多少信心？如果做不到，又該怎麼思考投資 AI 公司這件事？","我現在提出的檢驗是這樣：告訴我，你認為某家 AI 公司五年後的每股盈餘會是多少。**你願意賭最後的實際數字落在你的估計值正負 50% 以內嗎？**（例如你猜每股 10 美元，你有多大把握實際數字會介於 5 到 15 美元之間？）","問題六：資料中心的前景如何？","過去，資料中心是儲存資料、代管電腦應用程式和網站的數位倉庫。如今「資料中心」（或稱「AI 工廠」）一詞，已演變成指 AI 的工作場所。它們好比汽車工廠，是製造 AI 的地方，從儲存資訊、訓練模型，到提供系統使用時間（稱為「算力」）都在其中。資料中心是 AI 產業發展的關鍵環節。資料中心的興建，以及算力的運算，會受到實體和其他方面的限制。","AI 的未來令人興奮，但有些人可能偏好謹慎的參與方式。這就意味著透過實體基礎建設投資，而不是投資這項新興技術的開發商和供應商，做法類似賣鏟子給淘金客，而不是去猜哪個淘金客會挖到金子。理論上，這樣就不那麼需要判斷最終贏家是誰，因為不論誰贏，都還是得用到這些基礎設施。目前算力供不應求，更添資料中心的投資吸引力。不過，有幾項因素必須考慮：","一提到「基礎建設」，我們常會聯想到「磚頭與水泥」，正是這種實體的穩固感，讓資料中心看起來像較安全的 AI 選擇。但資料中心的成本中，建築本身（也就是「外殼」）只占相當小的一部分。晶片（也就是「GPU」）才占大部分成本，而這一塊（以及過時淘汰的風險）通常是由資料中心的使用者來承擔。","必須找到並買下大片建地，資料中心開發商還得跨過法規門檻，並面對居民「不要蓋在我家後院」的反對。","必須能透過現有光纖連線到使用推論服務的用戶。","資料中心的建造者必須處理電力、冷卻設備和營建服務可能出現的瓶頸。","資料中心可取得的資金有限，可能會限制興建的速度，未來專案的融資成本也可能出乎意料。","有意思的是，上述的潛在瓶頸可能讓有意興建資料中心的人日子難過，並限制其成長。但換個角度看，「算力」供給的成長若受到抑制（上週多地成功通過暫停興建資料中心的禁令，就隱含這一點），現有的、或將來能蓋成的資料中心就會更賺錢，價值也更高。","這裡的重點是：資料中心不是「穩穩得分的投籃」。聰明的投資人必須找出不受上述不確定性影響、也不會因過度興建而造成算力供給過剩的機會。就像所有類型的投資一樣，資料中心投資人有成功的，也有較不成功的。不過以 AI 的動態來看，我認為贏家的成功和輸家的失敗，可能比一般「基礎建設」更加懸殊。","問題七：我們對這個產業的異常依賴，需要擔心嗎？","今天，AI 在整個投資環境中占了不成比例的分量，為所有投資人（以及經濟中的所有參與者）帶來可觀的不確定性，而不只是投資 AI 的人：","AI 在美國經濟中只占一小部分，卻貢獻了不成比例的經濟成長。","AI 對 GDP 成長的貢獻，目前絕大部分來自資本支出，主要是興建資料中心。這部分快速攀升，其他的營建卻在衰退。","截至今年 6 月 30 日的連續 14 季，「七大巨頭」（多半是超大規模雲端業者）的盈餘成長速度，都快過標普 500 指數的其他成分股。","有些 AI 公司從投資其他 AI 相關公司的股權中獲得龐大收益，使它們成為標普 500 盈餘的最大貢獻者。FactSet 指出，第二季 Alphabet 和亞馬遜合計認列 1,510 億美元的股權投資收益，主要來自 Anthropic 和 SpaceX（現已併入 xAI）。","AI 對整體盈餘的影響並不止於這個產業。相關零組件的賣家同樣繳出強勁財報，AI 和 AI 周邊產業的員工把薪水花在房租、食物、汽車和其他與 AI 本身相距甚遠的事物上，也帶來連鎖效應。","AI 對股市漲幅的貢獻不成比例：根據摩根大通的資料，2024 年 1 月以來，標普 500 約三分之二的價格漲幅，來自 42 家 AI 相關公司。","這些趨勢加總起來，使標普 500 高度集中在少數幾家 AI 與科技公司。","關鍵的觀察是：今天我們的經濟與市場所依賴的因素相對缺乏分散。對 AI 的曝險，構成貫穿各種投資組合的一條格外強大的「斷層線」，有可能主宰投資環境的方方面面。晶片壽命、企業策略或算力需求出現負面發展，可能引發深遠後果。**毫無疑問，過度依賴單一產業，會讓經濟和股市比起由廣泛因素支撐時更加脆弱。**","1977 至 1978 年，麥可．米爾肯（Michael Milken）開創了投資級以下債券的發行，帶動整個投資業願意承擔風險，只要認為隨之而來的潛在報酬足以補償風險。他的論點建立在兩大基石上：（a）額外的殖利率有機會補償違約債券的損失，以及（b）**足夠的分散，讓少數幾檔違約不致毀掉整體投資組合的成績。**但今天，我們的股市和債市高度依賴單一因素：AI 的成功。","問題八：這對信用市場有什麼意涵？","根據高盛的資料，今年到目前為止，全球與 AI 相關的債券發行量接近 6,000 億美元，包括超大規模雲端業者發行的債券、資料中心／專案融資，以及其他與 AI 有關的借款人所發行的債券。其中約 4,000 億美元流入美國投資等級債市場，占投資等級債總供給的五分之一以上，使 AI 相關債券在彭博美元投資等級公司債指數中的占比升至約 12%。至於美國高收益債市場（超大規模雲端業者並不直接在此發行），占比則約為 6%。顯然，AI 相關的債務發行是今年資本市場的重大發展之一。","展望未來，AI 看來平均每年需要 1 兆美元的新資本，其中數千億美元很可能持續每年透過發債來籌措。AI 對債務資本的需求，會對利率造成什麼影響？","由於擴充產能和開發新產品對超大規模雲端業者和前沿實驗室而言是當務之急，而且它們不想看到競爭者冒出來，這些公司多半不可能只因為利率可能比預期高，就放慢對 AI 的投資。這表示它們非得成為融資的「接受者」不可，即使資金吃緊、成本上升也一樣。","過去，經濟學家常談到「排擠效應」，指政府大舉借債，使民間部門難以籌到所需的債務資本。目前我覺得，對利率不敏感、願意不計代價的超大規模雲端業者所產生的債務需求，有可能把政府必須支付的利率推高。我們迄今看到的利率上升，不是正與此有關嗎？","同樣地，股票市場有能力每年提供這些公司所需的數千億美元新股權投資，來補足它們的現金流嗎？過去十年中有八年，美國傳統 IPO 的募資額都低於 500 億美元（例外是 2020 年的近 800 億美元，以及榮景年 2021 年的 1,400 億美元）。我懷疑任何一年可供運用的新資本就是那麼多；AI 對債務與股權的胃口，最終不會為了爭奪這些資本而互相排擠嗎？","過去，超大規模雲端業者那些高獲利的非 AI 業務創造了龐大的自由現金流，讓它們沒有負債、現金充裕，信用評等也很高。現在情況不同了，這些公司正在花掉現金庫存和當期的現金流，同時借更多的錢。它們強勁的投資等級評等，讓它們輕易就能為 AI 擴張籌資。但隨著這個過程持續下去，它們的信用體質和（或）評等會不會改變？對於今天憑著高評等放款、卻眼看這些公司提高槓桿的債權人，可能有什麼影響？","目前部分資料中心的融資，是靠超大規模雲端業者承租人的投資等級評等促成的。另一些債務則以晶片作為擔保品。但投資人必須非常留意某些資料中心租約中的「退出條款」，讓承租人或保證人在特定條件下（例如中心未能達到 99.9% 的可靠度）終止其義務；也要留意以晶片作為擔保品，可能撐不到債務到期的潛在風險。","業界所謂的「循環交易」，也就是公司出錢讓別人買自己的產品，有什麼影響？我們常看到晶片製造商、超大規模雲端業者、前沿實驗室和資料中心建造商之間互相放款又互相採購。有時資金從 A 流向 B，再流回 A；有時從 A 到 B，再成為給 C 的融資，而 C 又拿去向 A 採購。這些交易是各方在公平的獨立立場下願意進行的嗎？還是供應商在補貼買家，以支撐需求、保護自家產品的市占率？這種互相依賴帶來哪些風險？此外，特殊目的實體的借款不計入資產負債表，這對超大規模雲端業者的信用體質有什麼影響？母公司到底是不是這些債務的後盾？","最後，在一項令人興奮的技術進步所引發的狂熱時期借款，會有什麼效應？最終，我們看到幾項因素匯聚在一起：","AI 公司極度渴望借錢，","投資人對 AI 感到興奮，因此願意放款，","如前所述，資本市場高度集中於 AI，以及","AI 產業參與者之間所謂的「循環交易」。","AI 的貸款人面對的是一個新時代，特徵是債務集中在單一產業、產業基本面快速變動，以及高度仰賴未來算力銷售收入的水準。**貸款人必須對自己支持的借款人信用體質，以及放款的結構與條件格外警覺。**","問題九：AI 有多可預測？這些問題能得到多少解答？","我在備忘錄和我的書《掌握市場週期》中寫過，對於想要因應未來的人來說，只有預測是不夠的。除了預測之外，你還需要對這個預測正確的機率有個看法。我認為就 AI 而言，鑒於它的技術複雜性、前所未有的性質和變化速度，這個機率異常地低。","我想把 AI 的可預測性量化，但擔心這遠超出我的能力。於是我請 Claude 幫忙，以 1（無從得知）到 10（完全可預測）的分數替 AI 評分。它的回答很有啟發性。Claude 就這個主題做了總結，反映出上面討論的一些不確定性（強調為本文所加）：","**我的整體判斷：AI 整體約得 3 分。**這個數字掩蓋了一種分裂。方向相當可預測，大概是 6 或 7 分：這項技術會持續進步，並擴散到整個經濟體。但投資人真正賺錢所依賴的事情就難預測得多，大約 2 分：事情發生得多快、利潤有多大、又是誰賺走這些利潤。","我也請 Claude 替 AI 產業的各個子領域評定可預測性，並指出非 AI 經濟中它認為在這方面相當的部分。以下是它所謂的「判斷」：","下表列出 Claude 對 AI 各子產業的可預測性評分（1 = 無從得知，10 = 完全可預測），以及它認為可預測性相當的傳統產業。","令我意外的是，前沿實驗室只拿到 2 分，我的理解是「幾乎無從得知」。在我看來，這些是令人印象深刻、領先幅度很大的公司，但 Claude 指出：「這些事業近乎非黑即白。資本需求龐大，每單位能力的價格持續快速下跌，而且是否存在能長久維持的護城河並不清楚。」（Claude 屬於 Anthropic，這不是很諷刺嗎？）耐人尋味的是，照 Claude 的說法，這些最不確定的標的，在私募市場上似乎被賦予溢價。OpenAI 和 Anthropic 都已申請 IPO，它們的股票在公開市場如何定價、上市後表現如何，會很有看頭。還要注意，第二季推升標普 500 盈餘的股權收益（見第 6 頁的說明），大部分都和這個被形容為「最難預測」的領域有關；這些收益能持續多久？","最後談到可預測性，我想順便說幾句地緣政治，而在 AI 這個領域，地緣政治通常就是指「中國」。幾乎所有最先進的晶片都來自台灣的台積電。美國限制把最先進的晶片賣給中國，這促使中國推出低成本模型，以低價侵蝕性能更強的美國模型的銷售。如果這個局面改變，美國的競爭地位會如何？中國的晶片會追上或超越現今的領先者嗎？中國有沒有可能進而阻撓、甚至禁止台灣晶片銷往美國？還有，對中國意圖的疑慮，會不會削弱為 AI 產業設置安全護欄的努力？","很明顯，關於 AI，投資人有很多事不知道，也無法預測。**問題在於：你願意承擔多少風險、以什麼形式承擔？AI 產業有許多環節，而（依 Claude 的說法）它們的範圍，從我們大致一無所知的前沿實驗室，到有穩固租約的資料中心及其電力供應商，後者的可預測性大約和大型工業企業及受管制的公用事業相當。**","問題十：AI 是泡沫嗎？","對上述某些問題的擔憂，最近幾週開始浮現。投資環境中出現多項負面因素交織，圍繞著通膨、聯邦赤字與債務、由此推高的利率，以及伊朗戰爭似乎難以收場。除了這些一般性問題，又加上一些 AI 特有的考量，例如：","新模型的競爭：功能較少但更便宜的模型、低價的中國模型，以及可免費下載的開源模型；","每個「token」（模型使用量的計價單位）的價格下滑；","算力使用量成長可能放緩；","公布的銷售成長低於預期；以及","對部分公司槓桿水準偏高、並高度依賴未來融資的疑慮。","在順境時很容易被打發掉的問題，一旦投資人的心神不寧，就會讓人憂慮。於是我們發現，正如我常說的，現實生活中的事情是在「還不錯」與「不太好」之間擺盪，但在投資人心中卻是從「完美無瑕」一路跌到「毫無希望」。AI 投資人這幾天擔心的事並不是新鮮事，只是投資氣候改變，讓它們足以使氣氛轉為陰沉。","**儘管這個話題已經被討論了超過一年，包括我自己在內，人們還是不斷在問：這是泡沫嗎？**首先我們需要為泡沫下個定義，我想提出我的版本：非理性的狂熱，導致不審慎的行為、過度的投資，以及參與的價格過高。我認為 AI 泡沫，和任何圍繞技術進步的可能泡沫一樣，至少得從三個角度來檢視。**這項技術本身是不是被過度炒作、終將令人失望？公司投入的金額是不是過多、注定只會換來不足的報酬？投資人是不是承擔了太多風險，並為這個領域的股份付出太高的價格？**要對這些問題下結論，很大程度取決於前面那些問題的答案。我覺得自從我上次在 2 月寫 AI 備忘錄以來，這幾方面沒有太大變化：","我仍然認為 AI 本身會證明是強大且影響深遠的，將以我們連想像都想像不到的方式，改變企業、經濟和廣義的生活。所以不，這項技術不是幻覺。","外界有大量討論，談的是未來必須創造多少營收，才能證明資本支出的合理性。但在我們了解 AI 會被怎麼使用、誰會從中獲利之前，沒辦法評估這些公司花錢是否明智。","而在這些知識讓我們能合理估計公司的盈餘和基礎建設的獲利能力之前，我們也沒辦法判斷股票的價格、或債券提供的殖利率是否恰當。","圍繞 AI 有很多疑問盤旋（部分已在上文描述），但關鍵可以歸結為這個相當複雜的問題：**AI 能不能為使用者提供足夠的價值，足以支撐算力的銷售量和夠高的算力價格，從而替投入其中的數兆美元（以及是哪些公司的資金）帶來足夠的報酬？**","和我認為見多識廣的人交談後，我得到的結論是：就現階段而言，回答這個問題在很大程度上是信仰問題。這讓我想起美國經濟學家兼作家斯圖爾特．蔡斯（Stuart Chase）的一句話：","對於相信的人，不需要任何證明；對於不相信的人，任何證明都不可能。","不管我怎麼努力，都想不出比這句話更貼切的方式，來回答圍繞 AI 的這些問題。","問題十一：該如何思考投資 AI？","我認為，人工智慧是至少一個世代以來，對投資人而言影響最深遠、也最難預測的發展。**但投資人不需要只因為它不確定，就把它拋在一旁。**正確的回應不是僵住不動，而是（a）在策略選擇、部位大小與承擔風險上，保持經過校準的謙卑，以及（b）在不確定中仍願意採取行動。","投資 AI 的不同面向，需要回答不同的問題。投資前沿實驗室股權的人，可能需要同時看對技術進展、競爭定位、定價能力、未來獲利和估值。為長期合約下的資料中心提供融資的貸款人，需要看對的事情可能比較少，但關鍵的幾件事（承租人的信用體質、合約條款、電力供應與殘餘資產價值）卻截然不同。因此，一個關鍵的問題是：「在這些問題中，我或我的機構擅長回答哪些？在缺乏這方面專業的情況下，我願意承擔哪些不確定性？」","在其他條件相同下，只要投資的價格相對於內在價值夠低，投資人就可以接受高度的不確定性。至少在理論上，在 Claude 的可預測性量表上評分較低的領域，只要比可預測性較高的領域便宜得夠多，就可能是更好的投資標的。歸根究柢，聰明的投資在很大程度上是價格問題。**不過請注意，就 AI 來說，上述各項問題讓我們很難思考內在價值，也就很難判斷是否便宜。**","為了把這一切串起來，最後我留給各位一份問題清單，或許有助於你做出決定。不過請注意，這些問題適用於所有投資，不只是 AI：","要賺到可以接受的報酬，有多少事情必須順利？","如果我的核心假設被證明是錯的，我最多會賠多少？","我承擔的風險中，哪些有得到補償，哪些只是我平白承受？","價格中已經反映了多少樂觀預期？","這項投資如何改變我整個投資組合的風險輪廓？它是放大我已經承擔的風險，還是分散了風險？","有一句老話，似乎非常適用於 AI：「有兩種投資人會賠很多錢：一種是什麼都不懂的人，另一種是什麼都懂的人。」我認為就 AI 而言，特別重要的是接受自己並非無所不知。AI 一定會帶來許多意外，但不見得無法承受。該思考的是：一項 AI 投資讓人失望的機率有多高……而不讓人失望的機率又有多高？","最後，我要以前幾天偶然看到的一段精彩引言作結：","**［有人］主張，近期的問號實在太多，何不等局勢明朗一點再說？**你知道那套說詞：「在目前的不確定因素解決之前，先保留買進的資金，等等。」在拿起這根拐杖之前，請先面對兩個令人不舒服的事實：未來從來都不明朗；而在股市裡，一片樂觀的共識，你得付出非常高的代價。不確定性其實是長期價值買家的朋友。（強調為本文所加）","拿來為一篇談 AI 的備忘錄收尾，是不是再完美不過？","不過請注意，這段話出自我最古老的檔案之一：1979 年 8 月 6 日刊登在《富比士》雜誌上、華倫．巴菲特的永恆智慧。**我總是很樂意讓華倫說最後一句話。**"],"notes":{"4":"「由上而下」是先預測總體經濟，再往下推到產業、公司、市場。四個環節各猜對的機率是 60%，全部都對的機率是 0.6×0.6×0.6×0.6 ≈ 13%。環節一多，機率就快速縮水，這是 Marks 質疑預測能力的核心算式。","5":"他把同樣的邏輯套到 AI：要看對的變數有技術、算力需求、競爭、受益者、融資、時間點，共六項。就算每項都有 60% 把握，六項全中的機率約 5%（0.6 的六次方 ≈ 4.7%）。","6":"三個重點：永遠不可能知道全部；最大的錯誤出現在自以為全懂的時候；但也不能因為不確定就完全不投資。後面的結論「謙卑但仍行動」就是在呼應這裡。","11":"「可解釋性」（interpretability）是研究 AI 內部運作的領域。Marks 把它比成「手上有每一筆交易紀錄，卻不知道價格為什麼動」，意思是資料完整不等於理解。","12":"方括號［　］內是 Marks 自己插入的評論：他覺得 Claude 說「我們」時像個人，所以特別標注。","19":"「第二層思考」是 Marks 的招牌觀念：第一層只看表面（好公司＝好股票），第二層會問「這已經反映在價格裡了嗎？」。他在這裡問 AI 能不能做到。","23":"AGI（通用人工智慧）指能做人類能做的一切。Marks 追問的是標準到底設在哪裡：一般人、前 10% 的人，還是全世界最聰明的人。","26":"「大宗商品化」是指產品沒有差異、只能比價格，利潤會被壓到很薄，如航空、鋼鐵。Marks 引用巴菲特的看法：改變世界的技術（如網際網路）不代表投資人能賺錢，因為好處常流向消費者。","28":"邏輯是：AI 取代員工 → 政府少了所得稅收入 → 可能轉而對 AI 課稅。這是 Marks 的推測性問題，並不是已經存在的政策。","31":"「超大規模雲端業者」指擁有巨量運算資源的大型雲端公司。這段的核心是：進步速度快的產業，領先者也可能很快被取代。","32":"這是「倖存者偏差」的反面提醒：現在看起來理所當然的贏家（微軟、亞馬遜），當年並不是人人都看得出來，而 Napster、雅虎、Netscape 都曾風光一時。","43":"資料中心裡的晶片如果幾年就過時，帳上折舊就要攤得很快，每年認列的費用變高、獲利變低。這是評估 AI 公司獲利能力時很關鍵、卻常被忽略的假設。","48":"這是 Marks 提出的「五年盈餘檢驗」：如果你連「五年後每股盈餘落在 ±50% 以內」都不敢賭，那就代表這項預測的不確定性太大。","51":"「賣鏟子給淘金客」是淘金熱時賺錢的經典故事：賣工具的人比挖金子的人穩賺。對應到 AI 就是投資基礎設施，而不是押注哪家模型公司會贏。後面的段落再說明這招並沒那麼穩。","52":"資料中心的成本大部分是晶片（GPU），不是建築。晶片會過時，這個風險通常由租用中心的使用者承擔，所以「磚頭與水泥」的穩固感其實只是錯覺的一部分。","57":"供給受限會讓現有資料中心更值錢（稀缺性），這和前一段的「瓶頸讓蓋的人日子難過」是同一件事的兩面：蓋的人吃虧，已經蓋好的人受益。","58":"「layup」是籃球的上籃，指幾乎一定進的球。Marks 的意思是：資料中心沒有那麼穩當的好事。","64":"股權收益是帳面上的投資利益（例如所投資公司估值上升），它會墊高 S&P 500 的盈餘，但品質不見得和營運獲利一樣穩定。","68":"「斷層線」比喻潛在的巨大風險：平時看不出來，一旦錯動，整個投資組合都會受影響。分散投資的意義，就是避免所有部位押在同一條斷層上。","69":"Milken 在 1970 年代末開創垃圾債（高收益債）市場：用較高的殖利率補償部分違約，再靠分散讓少數違約不致影響整體。Marks 的重點是：這套邏輯需要「多種不同的風險來源」，但現在大家都押同一個因素 AI，分散就失效了。","71":"「投資等級」是信用評等 BBB 以上的債券；「高收益債」則是 BB 以下，俗稱垃圾債。AI 債券占投資等級指數的 12%，代表許多一般投資人已不知不覺間大量曝險在 AI 上。","74":"排擠效應原本指政府借太多錢，讓民間借不到錢、利率被推高。Marks 認為現在換成 AI 公司的巨額借款扮演同樣的角色：它們「不管利率多高都要借」，把全市場的利率往上拉。","75":"意思是市場每年能提供的新資本有限（IPO 募資多半低於 500 億美元），AI 一年要上千億美元，債務與股權兩邊就得搶同一池資金。","77":"「退出條款」讓承租人在特定條件下（如可靠度低於 99.9%）可以終止租約。如果貸款的還款來源就是這份租約，退出條款等於讓債權的保障打折扣。","78":"循環交易的例子：晶片廠投資或借錢給 AI 實驗室，實驗室再拿這筆錢去買該晶片廠的晶片。營收看起來很漂亮，但真實的獨立需求可能沒那麼大。「公平的獨立立場」指雙方像陌生人一樣、各自獨立議價所達成的交易。","86":"Marks 的重要觀念：預測不夠，還要估計「預測正確的機率」。他認為 AI 的這個機率特別低。","88":"Claude 的區分很有意思：「方向」（AI 會變強、會普及）容易預測，但「投資人賺錢所依賴的事」（速度、利潤、誰賺走）很難預測。看對方向不等於賺得到錢。","91":"「近乎非黑即白」（binary）是說結果只有大成功或大失敗兩種。一般來說，越難預測的資產，價格應該越便宜才合理，但 Marks 觀察到私募市場反而給了溢價。","101":"這是 Marks 常說的市場心理：現實是在「還不錯」與「不太好」之間擺盪，但投資人的情緒卻在「完美」與「絕望」兩個極端之間跳。","102":"Marks 對泡沫的定義：非理性的狂熱，導致不審慎的行為、過度投資與過高價格。他用三個問題來檢查：技術被炒作了嗎？投資過頭了嗎？投資人付太高價格了嗎？","108":"意思是這已是信仰之爭：信的人不需要證據，不信的人給再多證據也不會信服。Marks 用它說明現階段 AI 前景很難用資料說服彼此。","111":"他的結論是「不是僵住不動，而是校準過的謙卑加上行動意願」：承認不確定、控制部位大小，但還是要下場。","113":"不確定性可以靠「價格夠低」來補償：越難預測的標的，越要求更大的折價（安全邊際）。這是價值投資的核心。","120":"老話的重點是「自以為什麼都懂」和「什麼都不懂」一樣危險。AI 是會一再出現驚喜的領域，所以要預留承受意外的空間。","122":"這是巴菲特 1979 年的一句名言：未來從來都不明朗，等到明朗時價格早已反映，你得付出更高的代價。所以不確定性反而是長期價值買家的朋友。"},"vocab":[{"w":"abstain","pos":"v.","ipa":"/əbˈsteɪn/","zh":"戒除；棄權；避免","exp":"abstain from + 名詞／動名詞。投資語境：abstain from investing 表示刻意不碰某類資產。","ex":"investors don’t have the option to abstain from thinking about it","exzh":"投資人沒有「不去想它」這個選項。","b":1},{"w":"deliberations","pos":"n.","ipa":"/dɪˌlɪbəˈreɪʃənz/","zh":"深思熟慮；審議","exp":"常用複數，指反覆權衡後的想法。deliberate 是動詞「仔細考慮」。","ex":"I’m going to share my deliberations","exzh":"我要分享我的思考。","b":2},{"w":"gyrating","pos":"v.","ipa":"/ˈdʒaɪreɪtɪŋ/","zh":"（劇烈）來回震盪","exp":"原指旋轉，金融新聞中常形容價格大幅上下震盪。","ex":"security prices for AI and AI-adjacent companies gyrating up and down","exzh":"AI 及相關公司的股價上下劇烈震盪。","b":2},{"w":"cope","pos":"v.","ipa":"/koʊp/","zh":"應付；因應","exp":"cope with 是常見搭配；這裡 Marks 說投資人「靠預測來應付」未來的不確定。","ex":"most investors cope by relying on forecasts","exzh":"多數投資人靠預測來應付。","b":3},{"w":"top-down","pos":"adj.","zh":"由上而下的","exp":"top-down investing 是先判斷總體經濟再選產業與個股；相對的是 bottom-up（由下而上，先研究個別公司）。","ex":"people who engage in top-down investing","exzh":"採取由上而下投資法的人。","b":4},{"w":"thesis","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˈθiːsɪs/","zh":"論點；投資論述","exp":"investment thesis 指你為什麼看好一項投資的整套理由。","ex":"the combined probability that any thesis is correct","exzh":"任何一套論點同時成立的機率。","b":5},{"w":"compute","pos":"n.","ipa":"/kəmˈpjuːt/","zh":"算力；運算資源","exp":"在 AI 語境中是名詞，指訓練和執行模型所需的運算能力，如 demand for compute。","ex":"the demand for compute","exzh":"對算力的需求。","b":5},{"w":"granular","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˈɡrænjələr/","zh":"細部的；顆粒度細的","exp":"get more granular 是「談得更細節」，常用於分析報告。","ex":"Before getting more granular, I’m going to start with a big-picture","exzh":"在進入更細節之前，我先從宏觀問題談起。","b":8},{"w":"interpretability","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ɪnˌtɝːprɪtəˈbɪləti/","zh":"可解釋性","exp":"AI 領域指理解模型內部為何做出某個輸出的能力。","ex":"Researchers in “interpretability” are making real progress.","exzh":"可解釋性研究人員正取得實質進展。","b":11},{"w":"extrapolating","pos":"v.","ipa":"/ɪkˈstræpəleɪtɪŋ/","zh":"外推；推測","exp":"把過去趨勢往未來延伸。投資人常犯的錯是 extrapolate recent trends（把近期趨勢無限外推）。","ex":"identifying historical patterns, extrapolating them, and using this ability","exzh":"找出歷史規律，並把它們外推，再運用這項能力。","b":15},{"w":"daunting","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˈdɔːntɪŋ/","zh":"令人卻步的","exp":"形容任務艱鉅、讓人望而生畏。a daunting task。","ex":"both stimulating and daunting","exzh":"既刺激又令人卻步。","b":16},{"w":"sycophancy","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˈsɪkəfənsi/","zh":"諂媚；阿諛奉承","exp":"AI 領域指模型一味附和使用者、說對方想聽的話。形容詞 sycophantic。","ex":"Can it give us skepticism rather than sycophancy?","exzh":"它能給我們懷疑，而不是一味奉承嗎？","b":18},{"w":"furnish","pos":"v.","ipa":"/ˈfɝːnɪʃ/","zh":"提供；供應","exp":"furnish A with B 或 furnish B，較正式，意同 provide。","ex":"Can it be instructed to furnish primary sources","exzh":"能否指示它提供第一手資料來源？","b":21},{"w":"entrusted","pos":"v.","ipa":"/ɪnˈtrʌstɪd/","zh":"託付","exp":"entrust A to B ＝把 A 託付給 B，帶有信任與責任的意味。","ex":"what work will be entrusted to AI","exzh":"哪些工作會被託付給 AI。","b":24},{"w":"transformative","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/trænsˈfɔːrmətɪv/","zh":"具變革性的","exp":"形容足以徹底改變產業或社會的事物，如 transformative technology。","ex":"transformative technologies don’t necessarily reward investors","exzh":"具變革性的技術未必讓投資人受益。","b":26},{"w":"commodity","pos":"n.","ipa":"/kəˈmɑːdəti/","zh":"大宗商品；無差異化的商品","exp":"商業語境中指沒有差異、只能比價格的產品。動詞 commoditize ＝使商品化。","ex":"will their service become an undifferentiated commodity that sells on the basis of price","exzh":"它們的服務會不會變成只能靠價格競爭的無差異商品？","b":26},{"w":"monopolies","pos":"n.","ipa":"/məˈnɑːpəliz/","zh":"壟斷企業；獨占","exp":"單數 monopoly。壟斷者有定價權（pricing power），因此獲利豐厚。","ex":"acquire capabilities that make them monopolies","exzh":"取得足以讓它們成為壟斷者的能力。","b":27},{"w":"moats","pos":"n.","ipa":"/moʊts/","zh":"（企業的）護城河","exp":"Buffett 提出的比喻：品牌、成本優勢等能長期保護獲利、擋住競爭者的優勢。","ex":"How strong are the moats surrounding today’s leaders?","exzh":"今天這些領導者的護城河有多深？","b":30},{"w":"forge ahead","pos":"phr. v.","zh":"奮力前進；搶先","exp":"forge 原指鍛造，forge ahead 表示穩定而用力地向前推進。","ex":"If companies can forge ahead that fast in the world of AI","exzh":"如果公司在 AI 世界能那麼快地衝到前面。","b":31},{"w":"supplanted","pos":"v.","ipa":"/səˈplæntɪd/","zh":"取代（通常指取而代之）","exp":"比 replace 更正式，常指新事物把舊事物排擠掉。","ex":"can they likewise be supplanted by others","exzh":"它們同樣可能被別人取代嗎？","b":31},{"w":"hyperscalers","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˈhaɪpərˌskeɪlərz/","zh":"超大規模雲端業者","exp":"指擁有巨量資料中心的大公司，如亞馬遜、微軟、Alphabet、Meta。","ex":"consider the vast resources and potential of the so-called “hyperscalers”","exzh":"想想所謂「超大規模雲端業者」的龐大資源與潛力。","b":31},{"w":"incumbents","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ɪnˈkʌmbənts/","zh":"現有的領先業者；在位者","exp":"指市場上已占有位置的公司，相對於 newcomers（新進者）。","ex":"Who will win among these incumbents?","exzh":"這些現有業者之中誰會勝出？","b":31},{"w":"fell by the wayside","pos":"phr.","zh":"半途被淘汰；被拋在路旁","exp":"fall by the wayside：在過程中失敗、被遺忘或放棄。","ex":"Napster, Yahoo, and Netscape fell by the wayside","exzh":"Napster、雅虎和 Netscape 被淘汰了。","b":32},{"w":"cash flows","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˈkæʃ floʊz/","zh":"現金流","exp":"公司實際流入流出的資金。價值投資常把未來現金流折現（discount）回現在來估值。","ex":"estimates a company’s earnings or cash flows in the future","exzh":"估計一家公司未來的盈餘或現金流。","b":34},{"w":"depreciation","pos":"n.","ipa":"/dɪˌpriːʃiˈeɪʃən/","zh":"折舊；貶值","exp":"把設備成本分攤到使用年限。壽命越短，每年折舊越高，獲利越低。","ex":"how fast will data-center depreciation have to be charged off","exzh":"資料中心的折舊必須多快攤提完。","b":43},{"w":"breakneck","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˈbreɪknek/","zh":"極快（甚至危險）的","exp":"breakneck speed／pace，形容快到令人擔心會出事。","ex":"today’s breakneck spending race","exzh":"今天這場狂飆的燒錢競賽。","b":44},{"w":"capital expenditures","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˈkæpətəl ɪkˈspendɪtʃərz/","zh":"資本支出（capex）","exp":"企業買設備、蓋廠房等長期投資的支出，常縮寫為 capex。","ex":"a decent return on today’s capital expenditures","exzh":"對今天的資本支出帶來像樣的報酬。","b":45},{"w":"conglomerates","pos":"n.","ipa":"/kənˈɡlɑːmərəts/","zh":"綜合企業集團","exp":"橫跨多種不相關行業的大公司，1960～70 年代很盛行。","ex":"analyzing the conglomerates in the 1970s","exzh":"在 1970 年代分析綜合企業集團。","b":46},{"w":"conjecture","pos":"n.","ipa":"/kənˈdʒektʃər/","zh":"臆測；推測","exp":"沒有確切證據的猜測。a matter of conjecture ＝只能猜的事。","ex":"companies that mostly consist of conjecture","exzh":"大半只是臆測的公司。","b":47},{"w":"frontier labs","pos":"n.","zh":"前沿（模型）實驗室","exp":"指開發最先進 AI 模型的公司，如 OpenAI、Anthropic。frontier 有「最前線」的意思。","ex":"leading frontier labs (let alone companies that mostly consist of conjecture)","exzh":"領先的前沿實驗室（更別說那些大半只是臆測的公司）。","b":47},{"w":"picks and shovels","pos":"n.","zh":"鎬與鏟（賣給淘金客的工具）","exp":"投資比喻：不賭誰找到金子，而是賣工具給所有淘金客賺錢。","ex":"selling picks and shovels to gold miners","exzh":"賣鏟子給淘金客。","b":51},{"w":"obsolescence","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˌɑːbsəˈlesns/","zh":"過時；淘汰","exp":"技術或設備被新產品取代而失去價值。形容詞 obsolete。","ex":"the risk of obsolescence","exzh":"過時淘汰的風險。","b":52},{"w":"borne","pos":"v.","ipa":"/bɔːrn/","zh":"承擔（bear 的過去分詞）","exp":"bear risk／cost 是「承擔風險／成本」，被動語態 is borne by。","ex":"is generally borne by the center user","exzh":"通常由資料中心的使用者來承擔。","b":52},{"w":"hurdles","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˈhɝːdlz/","zh":"門檻；障礙","exp":"原指跨欄；hurdle rate 則是投資人要求的最低報酬率。","ex":"overcome regulatory hurdles","exzh":"克服法規障礙。","b":53},{"w":"bottlenecks","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˈbɑːtlnek/","zh":"瓶頸","exp":"整個流程中限制產出速度的環節，如電力、冷卻設備。","ex":"potential bottlenecks in terms of the needed electric power","exzh":"電力方面可能出現的瓶頸。","b":55},{"w":"constrain","pos":"v.","ipa":"/kənˈstreɪn/","zh":"限制；制約","exp":"比 limit 正式，常用於「資源有限而制約了成長」。","ex":"could constrain the pace at which they’re constructed","exzh":"可能限制興建的速度。","b":56},{"w":"moratoriums","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˌmɔːrəˈtɔːriəmz/","zh":"暫停令；禁令","exp":"官方宣布在一段期間內暫停某活動，如暫停興建資料中心。","ex":"moratoriums on data center construction","exzh":"暫停興建資料中心的禁令。","b":57},{"w":"layup","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˈleɪʌp/","zh":"上籃；輕而易舉的事","exp":"籃球中近距離上籃，幾乎必進。比喻穩贏、輕鬆的機會。","ex":"data centers aren’t a “layup.”","exzh":"資料中心不是「穩穩得分的投籃」。","b":58},{"w":"insulated","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˈɪnsəleɪtɪd/","zh":"受保護而不受影響的","exp":"insulated from risk ＝與風險隔絕。原指絕緣、隔熱。","ex":"opportunities that are insulated from the uncertainties listed above","exzh":"不受上述不確定性影響的機會。","b":58},{"w":"oversupply","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˈoʊvərsəˌplaɪ/","zh":"供給過剩","exp":"供給大於需求，會讓價格下跌、報酬下降。","ex":"overbuilding and thus oversupply of compute","exzh":"過度興建因而造成算力供給過剩。","b":58},{"w":"disproportionate","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˌdɪsprəˈpɔːrʃənət/","zh":"不成比例的","exp":"份量或影響與規模不相稱。副詞 disproportionately。","ex":"AI is responsible for a disproportionate share of what’s going on","exzh":"AI 在發生的事情中占了不成比例的份額。","b":60},{"w":"outsized","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˈaʊtsaɪzd/","zh":"特別大的；超乎比例的","exp":"outsized returns（超額報酬）、outsized risk（過大的風險）都很常見。","ex":"responsible for an outsized part of economic growth","exzh":"貢獻了不成比例的一大塊經濟成長。","b":61},{"w":"notched","pos":"v.","ipa":"/nɑːtʃt/","zh":"取得；錄得","exp":"notch up ＝取得（分數、成績）。比 achieve 口語。","ex":"Alphabet and Amazon notched a total of $151 billion of gains","exzh":"Alphabet 和亞馬遜合計認列 1,510 億美元的收益。","b":64},{"w":"knock-on effects","pos":"n.","zh":"連鎖效應","exp":"一件事引發另一件事的間接結果。美式也說 ripple effects。","ex":"there are knock-on effects as AI workers and AI-adjacent workers spend their pay","exzh":"AI 與相關產業的員工把薪水花出去，也帶來連鎖效應。","b":65},{"w":"aggregate","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˈæɡrɪɡət/","zh":"合計的；總體的","exp":"aggregate demand（總需求）、aggregate earnings（整體盈餘）。","ex":"AI’s impact on aggregate earnings doesn’t stop with the sector.","exzh":"AI 對整體盈餘的影響並不止於這個產業。","b":65},{"w":"concentrated","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˈkɑːnsəntreɪtɪd/","zh":"集中的","exp":"concentration 是名詞。指持股或曝險集中在少數對象，風險隨之集中。","ex":"left the S&P 500 highly concentrated in a few AI and tech companies","exzh":"使標普 500 高度集中在少數 AI 與科技公司。","b":67},{"w":"fault line","pos":"n.","zh":"斷層線；潛在的分裂點","exp":"地質用語，比喻隱藏的、一旦錯動就會出大事的結構性風險。","ex":"an unusually powerful “fault line” that runs through portfolios","exzh":"貫穿投資組合的一條格外強大的「斷層線」。","b":68},{"w":"incontrovertible","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˌɪnkɑːntrəˈvɝːtəbl/","zh":"無可爭辯的","exp":"無法被反駁的，比 undeniable 更正式。","ex":"It’s incontrovertible that disproportionate reliance on a single sector","exzh":"毫無疑問，過度依賴單一產業。","b":68},{"w":"pioneered","pos":"v.","ipa":"/ˌpaɪəˈnɪrd/","zh":"開創；首創","exp":"pioneer 當動詞指第一個做出某事，開拓新領域。","ex":"When Michael Milken pioneered the issuance of sub-investment grade debt","exzh":"當麥可．米爾肯開創投資級以下債券的發行時。","b":69},{"w":"ushered in","pos":"phr. v.","zh":"開啟；帶來（新時代）","exp":"usher in a new era ＝迎來新時代，語氣較正式。","ex":"he ushered in an investment-industry-wide willingness to accept risk","exzh":"他帶動了整個投資業接受風險的意願。","b":69},{"w":"cornerstones","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˈkɔːrnərstoʊnz/","zh":"基石","exp":"建築的奠基石，比喻最重要的基礎。","ex":"His thesis rested on two cornerstones","exzh":"他的論點建立在兩大基石上。","b":69},{"w":"incremental","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˌɪŋkrəˈmentl/","zh":"額外的；漸進的","exp":"incremental yield ＝多出來的殖利率，用來補償額外承擔的風險。","ex":"incremental yield that potentially compensates for the losses","exzh":"有機會補償損失的額外殖利率。","b":69},{"w":"investment grade","pos":"adj.","zh":"投資等級的","exp":"信用評等在 BBB- 以上的債券，違約風險相對低。簡稱 IG；以下稱為 high yield（高收益）。","ex":"U.S. investment grade debt market","exzh":"美國投資等級債市場。","b":71},{"w":"high yield","pos":"adj.","zh":"高收益的","exp":"評等低於投資等級的債券，殖利率較高、風險較大，俗稱垃圾債。","ex":"For the U.S. high yield bond market","exzh":"至於美國高收益債市場。","b":71},{"w":"crowding out","pos":"n.","zh":"排擠效應","exp":"政府大量借款，使民間借不到錢或利率被推高。Marks 借用它來描述 AI 對資金的需求。","ex":"In the past, economists used to talk about “crowding out,”","exzh":"過去，經濟學家常談到「排擠效應」。","b":74},{"w":"cash hoards","pos":"n.","zh":"囤積的現金","exp":"hoard 是儲藏、囤積。cash-rich 指現金充裕的公司。","ex":"these companies are spending their cash hoards and current cash flow","exzh":"這些公司正在花掉囤積的現金和當期現金流。","b":76},{"w":"leverage","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˈlevərɪdʒ/","zh":"槓桿；負債程度","exp":"用借來的錢放大投資。leverage 高代表賺時賺更多，虧時也虧更多、更容易出事。","ex":"as the companies increase their leverage","exzh":"當這些公司提高槓桿時。","b":76},{"w":"collateralized","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/kəˈlætərəlaɪzd/","zh":"以擔保品擔保的","exp":"collateral 是擔保品，債務人違約時債權人可取走。","ex":"Other debt is collateralized by chips.","exzh":"另一些債務以晶片作為擔保。","b":77},{"w":"out clauses","pos":"n.","zh":"退出條款","exp":"合約中讓一方在特定條件下可以終止義務的條款。","ex":"“out clauses” in some data-center leases","exzh":"某些資料中心租約中的「退出條款」。","b":77},{"w":"circular transactions","pos":"n.","zh":"循環交易","exp":"資金從 A 流向 B 又流回 A，製造出看似強勁的需求，但實質需求可能被誇大。","ex":"the industry’s so-called “circular transactions,”","exzh":"業界所謂的「循環交易」。","b":78},{"w":"arm’s length","pos":"adj.","zh":"公平的獨立交易（雙方互不影響）","exp":"at arm’s length ＝雙方像陌生人一樣各自獨立議價，不受特殊關係影響。","ex":"on an arm’s length basis","exzh":"在獨立公平的基礎上。","b":78},{"w":"off balance sheets","pos":"phr.","zh":"表外；不列入資產負債表","exp":"把債務放在特殊目的實體（SPV）中，母公司帳上看不到，但風險仍可能回到母公司。","ex":"special-purpose vehicles that keep debt off balance sheets","exzh":"讓債務不列入資產負債表的特殊目的實體。","b":78},{"w":"exuberance","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ɪɡˈzuːbərəns/","zh":"狂熱；興高采烈","exp":"Greenspan 的名言 irrational exuberance（非理性繁榮）常用來形容泡沫心理。","ex":"borrowing in a time of exuberance surrounding an exciting technological advance","exzh":"在圍繞一項令人興奮的技術進步的狂熱時期借款。","b":79},{"w":"confluence","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˈkɑːnfluəns/","zh":"匯集；合流","exp":"原指河流匯合，比喻多個因素同時出現。","ex":"we have the confluence of several factors","exzh":"我們看到幾項因素匯聚在一起。","b":79},{"w":"vigilant","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˈvɪdʒɪlənt/","zh":"警覺的；提高戒備的","exp":"對潛在風險保持高度注意。名詞 vigilance。","ex":"Lenders will have to be exceptionally vigilant regarding the creditworthiness","exzh":"貸款人必須對信用體質格外警覺。","b":84},{"w":"creditworthiness","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˌkredɪtˈwɝːðinəs/","zh":"信用體質；償債能力","exp":"借款人還得起錢、守信用的程度，評等機構的主要評估對象。","ex":"exceptionally vigilant regarding the creditworthiness of the borrowers they back","exzh":"對自己支持的借款人信用體質格外警覺。","b":84},{"w":"undercut","pos":"v.","ipa":"/ˌʌndərˈkʌt/","zh":"以更低價削弱（對手）","exp":"開出更低價格搶走對手的生意。也可指削弱某項主張。","ex":"low-cost models that can undercut sales of more powerful U.S. models","exzh":"能以低價侵蝕性能更強的美國模型銷售的低成本模型。","b":92},{"w":"blunt","pos":"v.","ipa":"/blʌnt/","zh":"削弱；使變鈍","exp":"原指刀鈍，blunt the impact／efforts ＝削弱影響或力道。","ex":"will worries about China’s intentions blunt efforts to impose safety guardrails","exzh":"對中國意圖的疑慮會不會削弱設置安全護欄的努力？","b":92},{"w":"guardrails","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˈɡɑːrdreɪlz/","zh":"護欄；防護機制","exp":"原指路邊護欄，比喻防止出事的規則或機制。","ex":"impose safety guardrails on the AI industry","exzh":"為 AI 產業設置安全護欄。","b":92},{"w":"bells and whistles","pos":"n.","zh":"花俏的附加功能","exp":"形容產品上額外、非必要的功能。fewer bells and whistles ＝功能比較陽春。","ex":"cheaper models with fewer bells and whistles","exzh":"功能較少但更便宜的模型。","b":96},{"w":"equanimity","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ˌekwəˈnɪməti/","zh":"平靜；沉著","exp":"面對壓力時情緒穩定不動搖。投資人的 equanimity 被動搖，就會開始恐慌。","ex":"when their equanimity is shaken","exzh":"當他們的心神平靜被動搖時。","b":101},{"w":"imprudent","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ɪmˈpruːdnt/","zh":"不審慎的；輕率的","exp":"prudent（審慎的）的反義字。Marks 的泡沫定義用它形容行為。","ex":"irrational exuberance leading to imprudent behavior","exzh":"非理性的狂熱導致不審慎的行為。","b":102},{"w":"overhyped","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˌoʊvərˈhaɪpt/","zh":"被過度炒作的","exp":"hype 是誇大宣傳。overhyped 指名氣和期待遠超過實際價值。","ex":"Is the technology itself overhyped and likely to disappoint?","exzh":"這項技術本身是不是被過度炒作而終將令人失望？","b":102},{"w":"fated","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˈfeɪtɪd/","zh":"注定的","exp":"be fated to do 表示命運已定，語氣比 destined 略帶悲觀。","ex":"excessive and fated to produce inadequate returns","exzh":"過多且注定只會產生不足的報酬。","b":102},{"w":"warrant","pos":"v.","ipa":"/ˈwɔːrənt/","zh":"值得；證明有理由","exp":"warrant 當動詞指「使某事成為合理」，常用於 warrant attention、warrant investment。","ex":"provide sufficient value to users to warrant sales of compute","exzh":"為使用者提供足夠價值，以支撐算力的銷售。","b":106},{"w":"consequential","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˌkɑːnsɪˈkwenʃl/","zh":"影響重大的","exp":"指後果重大、影響深遠，比 important 更強調「影響」。","ex":"the most consequential and least predictable development for investors","exzh":"對投資人而言影響最深遠、也最難預測的發展。","b":111},{"w":"paralysis","pos":"n.","ipa":"/pəˈræləsɪs/","zh":"癱瘓；僵住不動","exp":"analysis paralysis ＝分析過度而遲遲不行動。","ex":"The right response isn’t paralysis","exzh":"正確的回應不是僵住不動。","b":111},{"w":"calibrated","pos":"adj.","ipa":"/ˈkæləbreɪtɪd/","zh":"經過校準的；拿捏過的","exp":"calibrate 是校準儀器，比喻依實際情況調整判斷的程度。","ex":"calibrated humility regarding strategy selection, position sizing, and risk-taking","exzh":"在策略選擇、部位大小與承擔風險上，保持經過校準的謙卑。","b":111},{"w":"intrinsic value","pos":"n.","zh":"內在價值","exp":"一項資產依基本面（現金流、盈餘）應有的價值，和市場價格不一定相同。","ex":"if the price of the investment is low enough relative to its intrinsic value","exzh":"如果投資價格相對於內在價值夠低。","b":113},{"w":"amplify","pos":"v.","ipa":"/ˈæmplɪfaɪ/","zh":"放大；加強","exp":"讓某事更強烈。amplify risk ＝放大風險；相對於 diversify（分散）。","ex":"Does it amplify the risk I’m already carrying, or does it diversify it?","exzh":"它是放大我已經承擔的風險，還是分散了風險？","b":119},{"w":"crutch","pos":"n.","ipa":"/krʌtʃ/","zh":"拐杖；依賴的藉口","exp":"比喻不健康的依賴，如 use something as a crutch。","ex":"Before reaching for that crutch, face up to two unpleasant facts","exzh":"在拿起這根拐杖之前，先面對兩個令人不舒服的事實。","b":122},{"w":"goosed","pos":"v.","ipa":"/ɡuːst/","zh":"推升；刺激","exp":"goose 是口語，指推一把、使加速。goose earnings／sales ＝把數字拉高。","ex":"much of the equity gains that goosed S&P 500 earnings","exzh":"大部分推升標普 500 盈餘的股權收益。","b":91},{"w":"intractability","pos":"n.","ipa":"/ɪnˌtræktəˈbɪləti/","zh":"難以解決（的性質）","exp":"intractable 指頑固、難以處理的問題，如 intractable conflict。","ex":"the seeming intractability of the war in Iran","exzh":"伊朗戰爭似乎難以收場。","b":95}]};
